--TEST--
`Core\Socket::upgrade`'s `grants:` narrows the connection the way `spawn script`'s narrows a child: the connection holds a named capability the request holds, and loses one the request holds but the list does not name —`rule:concurrency/an-upgrades-options-are-spawn-scripts`, `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing` and `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`
--HEADERS--
Upgrade: websocket
Connection: Upgrade
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The server holds both. Every refusal below is a `grants:` list taking one away.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true

[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
--FILE note.txt--
note
--FILE note.nvs--
<?nvs
echo "unreachable\n";
--FILE chat.nvs--
<?nvs
// Named in `grants:` and held by the request, so the read answers.
echo "connection read ", Core\IO::read('./note.txt');

// Held by the request but not named, so the connection is refused at the door
// the request passed.
try {
    spawn script "note.nvs";
    echo "unreachable\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    echo "connection: ", $denied->message, "\n";
}
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The request keeps everything it narrowed for its connection. With no
// `--peer`, the connection's client closes at once and the script still runs.
Core\Socket::upgrade("chat.nvs", grants: ["fs.read"]);
echo "request read ", Core\IO::read('./note.txt');
--EXPECTF--
request read note
closed: 1000 closing
connection read note
connection: `spawn script` needs the capability `script.spawn` for %s%enote.nvs, which is not granted
help: add `spawn = ["%s"]` under `[capabilities.script]` in nvs.toml
